{
  "policy_type": "rules",
  "name": "policy_ep_40",
  "rules": [
    {
      "if": [
        {"feature": "vy", "op": "<", "value": -0.5},
        {"feature": "theta", "op": ">", "value": 0.1}
      ],
      "then": 1
    },
    {
      "if": [
        {"feature": "vy", "op": "<", "value": -0.5},
        {"feature": "theta", "op": "<", "value": -0.1}
      ],
      "then": 2
    },
    {
      "if": [
        {"feature": "vx", "op": ">", "value": 0.5},
        {"feature": "theta", "op": ">", "value": 0.1}
      ],
      "then": 3
    },
    {
      "if": [
        {"feature": "x", "op": "<", "value": 0.1},
        {"feature": "left_leg", "op": "==", "value": 1}
      ],
      "then": 0
    },
    {
      "if": [
        {"feature": "x", "op": "<", "value": 0.1},
        {"feature": "right_leg", "op": "==", "value": 1}
      ],
      "then": 0
    }
  ],
  "default_action": 0,
  "notes": "This policy draws inspiration from the successful behaviors observed in previous trajectories where the agent consistently managed to land safely when the vertical velocity (vy) was low and the lander angle (theta) was controlled. The first rule encourages firing the left orientation engine when descending rapidly while tilted right, based on successful landings with similar conditions. The second rule promotes using the main engine for rapid descents while tilted left, which is crucial for stabilizing the landing. The third rule addresses excessive horizontal velocity, prompting the use of the right orientation engine to correct course. The last two rules ensure that if the lander is close to the landing pad and one leg is already in contact, it does nothing to maintain stability. This new policy is expected to improve upon previous evaluations which had returns averaging around 50 with varied coverage, particularly in regions of high vertical descent." 
}

